course: dictionary
lesson: 7388
title: Plump: round and full
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Plump is a word for something round and full, and it is usually said in a kind way. A plump baby has soft, round cheeks, and plump strawberries are juicy and full of goodness.
explain: «A tardigrade, or water bear, is a plump little animal less than a millimetre long, lumbering about on eight stubby legs.» Plump tells you the shape: round and chubby, not thin. Lumbering and stubby add to the picture of a slow, sturdy little creature.
explain: Plump can also be a doing word, a verb. You can plump up a cushion by shaking it until it is round and soft again. Check which job the word does in the sentence: describing a thing, or an action.
fact: Plump means a little fat and round in a pleasant way.
fact: «Plump» is a kind word: a plump baby or a plump robin sounds sweet, not rude.
fact: «Plump» can also mean full and juicy, as in «plump strawberries».
fact: To plump up a cushion means to shake it until it is round and soft.
fact: Informal: to plump for something means to choose it after thinking, as in «I'll plump for the chocolate cake».
exercise: «The guinea pig is a plump cousin of mice and rats.» What does plump mean here? | Cold and wet | Bright and shiny | *Round and chubby in a pleasant way | Very thin and quick
exercise: Mum says, «Shake the cushion so it gets plump again.» What does plump up mean? | Throw it away | Sit on it heavily | Cut it open | *Shake it so it gets round and soft
exercise: Dad asks, «Which pudding will you plump for?» What does plump for mean? | *Choose it after thinking | Drop it on the floor | Make it smaller | Find it in the cupboard
test: «The raisins plumped up when we soaked them in water.» What does plumped up mean here? | Changed colour to red | *Became round, full and soft | Went dry and hard | Was thrown away
test: Which word means full and juicy, as in «plump strawberries»? | glum | treacherous | *plump | ancient
exam: Which sentence uses plump to mean round and chubby? | The robin sang at dawn | The bus was plump on time | *The plump robin puffed out its feathers in the snow
